1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,000
 [ Music ]

2
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
 >> That students get a better understanding of something if they know the history,

3
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
 know where it's come from.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 That's my view.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
 So when I go to class, when I go to the interface design class, for instance,

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 I can take them down to the museum and show them what an early interface was.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 We talk about interface design and they think of their handheld computer,

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
 their laptop, their desktop, and they look at the screen and they think that's an interface.

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
 Sure it is, but how did we get there?

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
 What was the early interface of computers?

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 And I take them down to the Ferranti series and they say, well,

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 what's the interface here with a computer?

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 So it's giving them the idea of what an interface is by taking them back to something

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 that looks very primitive and then we look at the evolution of interfaces over time.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 We look at the different applications.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 You know, we go back and look at PhysiCalc and WordStar and things like that

17
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 and sort of progress through the different versions of these different products.

18
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 You actually have like running PhysiCalc?

19
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 No, no, we don't.

20
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 No, no, I've sourced it from the web.

21
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 But I think it just broadens their outlook on IT

22
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 and I think it's a danger with computing students that they can become very focused on the latest

23
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
 and quickly discard the technology they're using when the next one comes along and forget about it.

24
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 Even before we had the museum, we were lucky. We had here SIRAC.

25
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 I've mentioned SIRAC to you, yeah, the world's number four digital stored program computer.

26
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 It's left over, used till about '65, so about '72 or so.

27
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 It arrived on campus just for storage purposes, but we put it in a display area.

28
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
 When I first arrived with my teaching here in about '88 to teach,

29
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,000
 I would always take my introduction to computer architecture students past.

30
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
 It had so much to tell students about the origin of operating systems,

31
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 the primary function of operating systems to allocate resources and the efficiency of use of resources.

32
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
 With the programming students, I can show them things like punch cards and things like this,

33
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 how do we get information into the computers and show them things like that.

34
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 It might be part of one class in a semester that I use the museum in particular for my classes,

35
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 and others use it too in similar ways, but we do have school children coming on visits,

36
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 and then we have about an hour tour and we take them through the museum,

37
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
 starting with the calculating machines, and we talk about what do people do before computers,

38
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
 and I've got things like this, what is a computer, where do we get the name of computing from,

39
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,000
 and I've got this picture of women using slide rules in 1948, I think it was,

40
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 this is in America, doing their calculations, and the idea that the first computers were people,

41
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
 and often they were women, so that's kind of an interesting bit of social history there.

42
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 So trying to connect the computers and state of the technology to what was happening in society at the time.

43
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
 The physical artefacts are very important to that.

44
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
 That idea of the physical, we still carry forward even today,

45
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
 as we're looking to see what we can do with the full museum that we've created now.

46
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 We're toyed with ideas of a web-based series of exhibitions, photographing all of the artefacts,

47
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 making sure that people can sort of browse through and move through halls.

48
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:14,000
 We feel that even with that web-based teaching methods, we would still like to have physical artefacts.

49
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,000
 We're toyed with a box of artefacts that we would send out to schools that are working on our program,

50
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
 and they do an educational program.

51
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
 So the physical hands-on is a very important thing.

52
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
 Museum curators understand it, people who aren't museum curators tend not to.

53
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
 We had a staff member in our school.

54
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
 When we started the museum, I was talking to her and said, "Oh, we will have slide wheels in there."

55
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:53,000
 Now she had done an honours degree in maths, and she had not heard, not that she hadn't seen a slide wheel,

56
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 she didn't even know what one was.

57
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 And that to me was amazing because when I went to uni, everyone carried a slide wheel.

58
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 This is my slide wheel from my university days.

59
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,000
 We all had these as science students, engineering students, we all had these tools.

60
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:19,000
 And she was probably ten years younger than me and suddenly had no idea what I was even talking about,

61
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:20,000
 hadn't heard the word.

62
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,000
 So I think that really made me realise that history is just so quickly forgotten,

63
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:36,000
 and that there's a danger that we forget about these things,

64
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 and if we don't have them around for people to see, they forget about the history of the technology that they're using.

65
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
 And I think that's a real danger.

